# Summary
Bug introduced: BucketOwnerEnforced only affects ACL acceptance on writes, but read authorization still honors public-read ACLs, allowing legacy public objects to remain accessible.
The commit introduces object-level public-read ACLs but the read-authorization path (`requester_can_read_object`) only checks the object's `public_read` flag and the public access block ignore flag. It never checks whether bucket ownership controls are set to BucketOwnerEnforced. While BucketOwnerEnforced blocks new public ACLs via `ensure_put_object_acl_supported`, existing public-read objects created before enabling ownership enforcement remain accessible to unauthenticated users. This is inconsistent with the expected semantics of BucketOwnerEnforced, which should disable ACLs entirely, and results in unintended information disclosure after administrators attempt to lock down a bucket.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Attempt debug build to enable runtime repro (failed due to missing libisal dependency).
- [x] Trace object read authorization flow and check for ownership-controls gating.
- [x] Verify write-time ACL enforcement for BucketOwnerEnforced and contrast with read-time checks.
- [x] Confirm ownership-controls update path does not clear existing object public_read metadata.
## Report
Attempted to build a debug binary for crash/ASan/valgrind reproduction with `cargo build -p server-core`, but the build fails because `ec-sys` requires libisal (see build_log in artifacts). Without a built binary, valgrind/asan/debugger runs were not possible.

Code review shows the read path uses `lock_object_for_authorized_read` which gates access via `requester_can_read_object` (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:2808-2833). `requester_can_read_object` grants access if the requester owns the object or if `object.public_read()` and public ACLs are not ignored; it does not check bucket ownership controls (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:2575-2587). BucketOwnerEnforced is only enforced on writes in `ensure_put_object_acl_supported`, which blocks unsupported ACLs during put (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:2677-2685). Enabling ownership controls just stores the config and does not clear existing object ACLs (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:3722-3741). This means legacy public-read objects remain readable even after BucketOwnerEnforced is set, matching the reported disclosure.

